A||||17||fierohoho||ATF in clutch hydraulic system follow up||||5||
Z||000000||fierohoho||07-08-2001||12:24 PM||||I have been reading a 5 page thread in the archives from the end of 1999 and was wondering what the people who put ATF inplace of DOT3 in their systems think after a year and a half. Any trouble over the long haul?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||07-08-2001||04:19 PM||||I followed that a while back and the general concensus was not to do it.  There seemed to be a lot of 'I wish I never...' posts.<BR>Gary||||reg||13
Z||000002||theogre||07-08-2001||05:40 PM||||Do This? Absolutuly Not!!!<P>One thing we all forgot when we started that is tha petrolium swells the rubber parts in the system. (Saddly, Many of us should have known better. Oil is a major problem that trashes brake parts.)<P>If you want to do anything, replace the DOT3 with something like Valvoline synthetic fluid which is supoesed to be less of a moisture magnet. It still absorbs water but not like DOT 3 does.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierohoho||07-08-2001||06:22 PM||||OK so what if anything do the guys who used motor oil of any type have to say?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||07-08-2001||10:14 PM||||I think I was one of the first to find out the ATF solution was no solution. After 900 miles the seals had swelled up so tight, the p/plate was no longer able to fully engage the clutch disk, it was fighting the pistons back into place.<P>What my solution was, and I haven't touched my clutch system in the 27,000 miles since I got rid of ATF. I bought brand new master and slave cylinders(again), put in another new clutch set, and poured in regular DOT3 brake fluid. I've never had a problem with the new parts and DOT3. I don't even think about my clutch system, as it has never had a problem since. I learned GM doens't pull these recommendations out of their ass, if the book says DOT3, you can bet I'm using DOT3.<P>Why use motor oil, why not put in what is recommended. If DOT3 isn't working, buy new cylinders.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-08-2001||10:23 PM||||Has anyone used DOT 5 silicone brake fluid? The problem is that DOT 3 and 4 brake fluid is hydroscopic (absorbs water) and water corrodes the cast iron and aluminum bores of slave and master cylinders. When the bores corrode they leak or fail.  Silicone or DOT 5 brake fluid does not absorb water.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||07-08-2001||11:45 PM||||i used the castrol product, i believe it is dot 5.  it has worked fine, but then i have only put 5-8000 miles on the car in a little over a year.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||07-09-2001||06:45 PM||||I used Valvoline syn about three months ago and have had no problems so far.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||07-09-2001||07:29 PM||||NO Petrolium Products in this system.. none period. no motor oil no ATF or anyhting else oil based. An no, you can't use synthetic motor/other oils either.<P>use DOT 3, Valvoline Synthetic, or an equivalent DOT 3/4 compatible fluid. (Valvoline claims their fluid is less of a water magnet then regular DOT 3/4 yet still readily disperses what water does enter.)<P>Assuming that because DOT 5 is non hydroscopic, and therefore better than glycol based DOT 3 or 4, is mistaken.<P>Any water that gets into DOT 3/4 will be disperesed thru the system Unlike Silicone DOT 5 in which the water will slug into low spots. DOT 3/4 family fluids contain inhibitors to deal with the water.<P>DOT 5 probably works better in a clutch than in brakes. (All the performance brake people, Baer, Wilwood, others, hate the stuff...) The clutch is probably less prone to aeration than the brakes.<P>Also,,, Something that many people are unaware. DOT 3/4 fluid leaves a lubricating film on the cylinder walls and pistons. It does this better than DOT 5. (As Quacker State GIrls says,,, It's Been Tested...) This lube film reduces wear of the parts.<P>Finally,,, having 2 diferant fluids in the car hydraulics can lead to very dangerous mixups. Much safer to stay with a single falimy of compatible fluids.||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||07-09-2001||08:53 PM||||Theogre you’re the man. Just one question though. How do you know so much about the automobile and its working parts? You're like a walking talking encyclopedia. Do you have one in front of you are something? Are you a Master Automotive Technician?||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||07-09-2001||08:54 PM||||Well, more than one question. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||07-09-2001||10:04 PM||||I been doing this way too long......  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||07-10-2001||08:47 AM||||don't do it.. if your wanting a fluid upgrad go to your local Ferrari or Alfa Romeo dealer and get Agip DOT 5.1.  some napas carry a similar fluid. it's about $30 a quart. and will take temps well in excess of 2000*. thats what i'm running in my braking system and that wha i put in the clutch system and brakes of my former 86 SE. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fsthydrantsig.jpg">         <BR>www.engineered.net||||reg||1
Z||000013||sparks||07-11-2001||02:34 PM||||I tried it in my '85 to get by for a while longer.  I knew it needed new cylinders but was too cheap to do it.<P>Bit me in the butt, it did!  I was out of town for a workshop when the clutch finally died on me.  If I had been at home I would've saved myself a large chunk of change putting in the new cylinders myself.  $600 later I drove home with a properly functioning clutch that feels great with DOT 3 brake fluid in it.<P>Problem was is swelled the diaphram in the cap of the master cylinder and allowed it to start sucking in air, well that was the last straw.  Really the problem was that the seals were worn and it needed new cylinders...<P>DON'T TRY IT!<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||11
Z||000014||theogre||07-11-2001||09:31 PM||||1fst,,<P>If you really feel your brakes get that hot.... Go for it. <P>I just can't justify $30 a quart for fluid. I can't justify the labor to fully purge the Glycol fluids from the system.<P>There are several fluids on the market that have boiling points over 500F and as high as 570F. All of those products are OK to use in systems previously filled with DOT 3 or 4. Valvoline synth boils over 500F, well above DOT 3 or 4. Baer for example recomends performance Friction Z rated and Castrol Racing Brake Fluids. Wilwood has their own high temp stuff.<P>I run Valvoline synthetic in my brakes. It's about $6 a quart. I'd use it in the clutch if I had one.<P>Unless "DOT 5.1" mixes with DOT 3 & 4. (DOT 5 Silicone does not.) There's no way I'd ever put it in my car or anyone elses without a total system overhaul.<P>The Valvoline is degraded by old fluid but the two will mix. You won't have slugs of wet fluid sitting in a caliper waiting to bite you.||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||07-12-2001||12:09 AM||||I've got the Valvolene synthetic in all my fieros, do you like the 5.1 agip better?<P>Is it a silicone fluid like "DOT 5" or a different beast?<P>Mix with the old fluids, or are we talking about "put in when you do the 13" ryane/held brakes and all new everything" :P)<P>What the heck, I stop by and oogle at Ferrari of SF about every 2 weeks anyway, they know me by now :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||StuGood||07-12-2001||07:06 PM||||I think that's about all I need to hear (or, read)...   DOT3 fer me, like it says on the cap of the clutch master cylinder.<P>I've been lucky enough not to have to deal with leaking hydraulics so far.  If / when that day finally comes, I'll remember what you all said here.  Thanks very much for the follow up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000017||theogre||07-12-2001||07:36 PM||||I've been working on clearing up confusion over the diferant fluids... Since I'm dealing mostly with street drive issues, I haven't seen much data on some of the really high temp products.<P>According to NHTSA. There are 4 seperate grades and 2 types of fluid.<P>Grades define boiling points, lubricity, and other characteristics.<P>Type is either Silicone of Non Silicone. (SBBF or non SBBF respectivly in FMVSS documents.)<P>DOT 3, DOT 4, and DOT 5.1 ard NON Silicone fluids. These are all clear to amber in color. DOT 5 is Silicone and is purple.<P>If you dig out the specs in the FMVSS stuff at this link.... <A HREF="http://www.access.gpo.gov/nara/cfr/waisidx_00/49cfrv5_00.html#501" TARGET=_blank>http://www.access.gpo.gov/nara/cfr/waisidx_00/49cfrv5_00.html#501</A> <P>The fluid gets "better" as the DOT number increases with an exception, DOT 5 and 5.1 have the same minimum dry boil temperature.<P>If you compare specs a bit further you'll find Valvoline Synthetic exceeds DOT 5 dry boil spec but is a bit lower than DOT 5.1 for wet boil. Valvoline's synthetic wet boil still out performs DOT 4's wet boil which puts it well above DOT 3.<P>The little other DOT 5.1 info I can find indicates that it can be flushed thru a dot 3/4 system without problem. I have not as yet found manufacturor recomendations regarding it.<P>Keep in mind, the FMVSS standards define the Minimum characteristics of the fluid. Now all but the cheapest products far exceed the DOT 3 standards. There are a number of "DOT 3" fluids that exceed DOT 4 spec easily in terms of boiling points.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-12-2001).]||||reg||4
